To implement this correctly, we will create a clean, three-layered architecture: the DTO (the data container), the Registry (the mapping instructions), and the Execution Engine (the logic).
1. The Data Container (DTO)
This is the object that will actually hold your data. For the TableA example, we use a Java record because it provides the required constructor and getters automatically.
package com.example.reports.dto;

public record TableADto(
    String colA1, 
    String colA2, 
    String colA3, 
    Integer colA10
) {}

2. The Mapping Registry (Metadata)
This is the "dummy" entity that stores your SQL instructions. You don't need to create a table for this in your DB; it’s purely for Hibernate's configuration.
package com.example.reports.entity;

import com.example.reports.dto.TableADto;
import jakarta.persistence.*;

@Entity
@SqlResultSetMappings({
    @SqlResultSetMapping(
        name = "TableAMapping",
        classes = @ConstructorResult(
            targetClass = TableADto.class,
            columns = {
                @ColumnResult(name = "colA1", type = String.class),
                @ColumnResult(name = "colA2", type = String.class),
                @ColumnResult(name = "colA3", type = String.class),
                @ColumnResult(name = "colA10", type = Integer.class)
            }
        )
    )
    // You can add TableBMapping, TableCMapping, etc., here in the same list
})
public class SqlMappingRegistry {
    @Id
    private Long id; // Not used, strictly for JPA compliance
}

3. The Execution Engine (Service)
This is the generic service that takes any SQL, a mapping name, and parameters to return your list of DTOs.
package com.example.reports.service;

import jakarta.persistence.EntityManager;
import jakarta.persistence.PersistenceContext;
import jakarta.persistence.Query;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;

@Service
public class QueryExecutionEngine {

    @PersistenceContext
    private EntityManager entityManager;

    @Transactional(readOnly = true)
    @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
    public <T> List<T> executeReport(String sql, String mappingName, Map<String, Object> params) {
        // 1. Create native query with the mapping 'contract'
        Query query = entityManager.createNativeQuery(sql, mappingName);

        // 2. Bind dynamic parameters securely
        if (params != null) {
            params.forEach(query::setParameter);
        }

        // 3. Execute and return mapped DTOs
        return query.getResultList();
    }
}

4. How to Use It (The Controller/Runner)
Here is how you would trigger the execution for TableA.
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/reports")
public class ReportController {

    @Autowired
    private QueryExecutionEngine queryEngine;

    @GetMapping("/table-a")
    public List<TableADto> getTableAReport() {
        // This SQL would typically come from your metadata table
        String sql = "SELECT colA1, colA2, colA3, colA10 " +
                     "FROM tableA " +
                     "WHERE colA1 = :val1 AND colA10 = :val10";

        // Dynamic parameters for the WHERE condition
        Map<String, Object> params = Map.of(
            "val1", "A",
            "val10", 100
        );

        // Execute using the registry key "TableAMapping"
        return queryEngine.executeReport(sql, "TableAMapping", params);
    }
}

Key Takeaways for Your Implementation
 * Order Matters: The order of @ColumnResult in your registry must match the order of fields in your DTO constructor (TableADto).
 * Case Sensitivity: Ensure the name in @ColumnResult matches the actual column names or aliases in your SQL string exactly.
 * Large Datasets: Notice the @Transactional(readOnly = true). This tells Hibernate not to track these objects for changes, which keeps your memory usage very low even when fetching millions of rows.
